B-0 (Boosty) audit round 2, F2 -- a pouch socket fd cannot live in an fd_set:
FD_SET stops instead of writing 128 MiB past the set, and ppoll() learns the
socket tag.

A pouch socket fd is 0x40000000 | slot (0006 / 0016). The dossier's central
obligation is that every fd-consuming call is tag-aware, and round 1's census
("every site that passes an fd to a raw syscall") concluded that every
remaining one FAILS VISIBLY. Two do not, and that census could not see either:
select takes a BITMAP and ppoll an ARRAY.

  FD_SET(sock, &set)   upstream's macro indexes fds_bits[d / 64] with no bound.
                       d = 0x40000000 is index 16,777,216 of a 16-long array:
                       a store 128 MiB past the set, in APPLICATION code,
                       before libc is entered. From the main stack that is
                       usually unmapped (a snare); from a heap-resident set it
                       is whatever Burrow sits there -- silent corruption.
                       select() does refuse the fd (n > FD_SETSIZE), but only
                       afterwards. FD_CLR / FD_ISSET are the same shape.
  ppoll()              was `syscall_cp(SYS_poll, fds, ...)`: a tagged fd
                       reaches the kernel as 0x4000000N, answers POLLNVAL,
                       counts as ready, defeats the timeout and busy-spins --
                       the exact failure 0015 fixed in poll() and not here.

Pre-existing, not introduced by this series; not reachable in-tree today
(tyrquake's FD_SET on a socket is under SERVERONLY; make's is a pipe fd). curl
-- the browser arc's likely network layer -- falls back to select() where
poll() is unusable.

THE FIX HERE is the honest minimum:
  - FD_SET / FD_CLR / FD_ISSET check that d fits the set and otherwise call
    __pouch_fdset_oob(), which abort()s. That is the check glibc's fortified
    build makes (__fdelt_chk): a stop replaces a silent wild store. Like
    glibc's it is made in the descriptor's FULL width against the set's OWN
    size -- `(unsigned long long)(d) < 8*sizeof(fd_set)`. The first version
    wrote `(unsigned)(d) < FD_SETSIZE` (audit r3 F3): a long 0x100000005
    passed as 5 and then indexed word 67,108,864, and FD_SETSIZE is read in
    the APPLICATION's macro context, so a port that redefines it to 8192
    weakened the bound over a 1024-bit set. POSIX lets these macros evaluate
    their arguments more than once.
  - __pouch_fdset_oob() says why ONLY when fd 2 is a terminal (audit r3 F4).
    fd 2 is routinely not stderr here -- a prover is spawned with {0,1};
    stratumd with no fds, so its fd 2 is its third real kernel handle -- and
    the first version wrote ~110 bytes of English at that handle's cursor.
  - ppoll() goes through poll(), which translates a tagged fd to its kernel
    poll target and drops negative fds.

THE REAL FIX is not here and is recorded as owed: socket fds that are small
integers (a placeholder kernel handle per slot with an fd -> slot side table),
which would let select() work on sockets and retire the tag from every
fd-consuming call. That is a redesign of 0006 / 0016 -- its own chunk, its own
audit. Until then a port that select()s on a socket stops (status 127, and on
a terminal a line that names the cause). What a port that poll()s gets is
0041's subject, not this patch's: this one only makes ppoll() REACH the
kernel's poll with the right handle. The same-day claim that such a port
"works" was false (audit r3 F1) -- the kernel sampled the server's end of the
connection for a client and walked no hook list when a reply arrived.

This patch puts BEHAVIOR into a public header, and the consequence is one no
other patch here has: the guard is compiled into the PORT's objects, not into
libc.a. (It is not the series' first public-header patch, as round 3's F8
disposition had it: 0001, 0003-0010 and 0021 edit arch/aarch64/bits/
syscall.h.in, installed as <bits/syscall.h>. Those add syscall NUMBERS;
this is the first to add code -- round 4 F6.) Relinking a port against the new sysroot
changes nothing; every port that uses FD_SET must be REBUILT to have it. In
this tree that happens by itself -- a stale sysroot is rebuilt from scratch and
build_sysroot removes build/pouch, every port's output with it -- which is why
this is a sentence and not a step. A port built OUTSIDE tools/build.sh against
an installed sysroot gets no such help. It is also why the macro must be warning-free in
every language mode a port compiles in -- checked as C99/C11/gnu17 and
C++11/17/20 under -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Wconversion -Wshadow -Wcomma.

The macros keep upstream's value (the assignment / the test) behind a comma:
`((void)(fits || (oob(d), 0)), <upstream expression>)`. A ?: form would make
every FD_SET statement in every port an unused-value warning.

Device-side pin (not in this patch file): pouch-hello-sockets re-runs itself as
a child that FD_SETs a socket fd and must die with abort's status (127), not
return and not fault; and it ppoll()s a connected socket, where a raw tagged fd
answers POLLNVAL. (That leg's first form asked for POLLIN with the reply "on
its way" and was decided by a scheduling race over the kernel defect above; it
is sequenced by barriers now and pins 0041 and the kernel fix as well.)

--- a/include/sys/select.h
+++ b/include/sys/select.h
@@ -23,10 +23,24 @@
 	unsigned long fds_bits[FD_SETSIZE / 8 / sizeof(long)];
 } fd_set;
 
+/* pouch: a descriptor that does not fit the set stops the program instead of
+ * indexing past it. Upstream's macros trust the caller, which is sound only
+ * while every fd is a small integer -- and a pouch SOCKET fd is not: it is
+ * 0x40000000 | slot, so FD_SET(sock, &set) was a store 128 MiB past the set,
+ * in application code, before libc was ever entered (select() refuses such an
+ * fd, but only afterwards). Sockets are waited on with poll(). The check is
+ * the one glibc's fortified build makes (__fdelt_chk), and like it is made in
+ * the descriptor's FULL width against the set's OWN size: `(unsigned)(d)`
+ * would let a long 0x100000005 through as 5, and FD_SETSIZE here would be
+ * whatever the application redefined it to. POSIX lets these macros evaluate
+ * their arguments more than once. */
+_Noreturn void __pouch_fdset_oob(int);
+#define __FD_FITS(d) ((unsigned long long)(d) < 8*sizeof(fd_set))
+
 #define FD_ZERO(s) do { int __i; unsigned long *__b=(s)->fds_bits; for(__i=sizeof (fd_set)/sizeof (long); __i; __i--) *__b++=0; } while(0)
-#define FD_SET(d, s)   ((s)->fds_bits[(d)/(8*sizeof(long))] |= (1UL<<((d)%(8*sizeof(long)))))
-#define FD_CLR(d, s)   ((s)->fds_bits[(d)/(8*sizeof(long))] &= ~(1UL<<((d)%(8*sizeof(long)))))
-#define FD_ISSET(d, s) !!((s)->fds_bits[(d)/(8*sizeof(long))] & (1UL<<((d)%(8*sizeof(long)))))
+#define FD_SET(d, s)   ((void)(__FD_FITS(d) || (__pouch_fdset_oob((int)(d)), 0)), (s)->fds_bits[(d)/(8*sizeof(long))] |= (1UL<<((d)%(8*sizeof(long)))))
+#define FD_CLR(d, s)   ((void)(__FD_FITS(d) || (__pouch_fdset_oob((int)(d)), 0)), (s)->fds_bits[(d)/(8*sizeof(long))] &= ~(1UL<<((d)%(8*sizeof(long)))))
+#define FD_ISSET(d, s) ((void)(__FD_FITS(d) || (__pouch_fdset_oob((int)(d)), 0)), !!((s)->fds_bits[(d)/(8*sizeof(long))] & (1UL<<((d)%(8*sizeof(long))))))
 
 int select (int, fd_set *__restrict, fd_set *__restrict, fd_set *__restrict, struct timeval *__restrict);
 int pselect (int, fd_set *__restrict, fd_set *__restrict, fd_set *__restrict, const struct timespec *__restrict, const sigset_t *__restrict);
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/select/__pouch_fdset_oob.c
@@ -0,0 +1,30 @@
+#include <sys/select.h>
+#include <stdlib.h>
+#include <string.h>
+#include <unistd.h>
+#include "syscall.h"
+
+/* FD_SET / FD_CLR / FD_ISSET on a descriptor that does not fit the set: see
+ * <sys/select.h>. Aborts -- the alternative was a silent wild store -- and
+ * says why ONLY when fd 2 is a terminal. fd 2 is routinely not stderr here (a
+ * prover is spawned with {0,1}; stratumd with no fds at all, so its fd 2 is
+ * its third real kernel handle), and ~110 bytes of English at that handle's
+ * cursor is a second bug. A raw write, no stdio: this can run in a program
+ * that links none. */
+_Noreturn void __pouch_fdset_oob(int d)
+{
+	static const char head[] = "pouch: fd 0x";
+	static const char tail[] =
+		" does not fit an fd_set (a socket fd is a tagged value here;"
+		" wait on it with poll())\n";
+	char hex[8];
+	unsigned u = d;
+	int i;
+	for (i = 7; i >= 0; i--, u >>= 4) hex[i] = "0123456789abcdef"[u & 15];
+	if (isatty(2)) {
+		__syscall(SYS_write, 2, head, sizeof head - 1);
+		__syscall(SYS_write, 2, hex, sizeof hex);
+		__syscall(SYS_write, 2, tail, sizeof tail - 1);
+	}
+	abort();
+}
--- a/src/select/ppoll.c
+++ b/src/select/ppoll.c
@@ -35,5 +35,9 @@
 		if (ms > 0x7fffffffLL) ms = 0x7fffffffLL;
 		timeout_ms = (int)ms;
 	}
-	return syscall_cp(SYS_poll, fds, n, timeout_ms);
+	/* Through poll(), not SYS_poll: poll() is where a pouch socket fd is
+	 * translated to its kernel poll target and a negative fd is dropped. Raw,
+	 * a tagged fd reaches the kernel as 0x4000000N, answers POLLNVAL, counts
+	 * as ready and busy-spins the caller. */
+	return poll(fds, n, timeout_ms);
 }
